/-! # A uniform bound on residue transcendence degrees after every base change

Source: Stacks Project, spaces-flat.tex, Tag 0CX1 (theorem-flat-dimension-n-representable),
proof, paragraph beginning “In particular the situation is local”; Tag 0CWS for charts
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-universal-fibre-transcendence-bound).

The bound precedes the choice of the test scheme and of its arbitrary étale chart.
The residue-field algebra is induced by the canonical chart projection to the test scheme.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace

/-- A finite type algebraic space with quasi-compact diagonal over an affine scheme has
one finite bound for the relative residue transcendence degrees on every étale chart
of every scheme base change. Charts are not required to be quasi-compact.
Source: Stacks Project, spaces-flat.tex, Tag 0CX1, bounded-fibre-dimension paragraph,
and Tag 0CWS (blueprint: lem-sfb-space-universal-fibre-transcendence-bound).

Proof:
1. Write the affine base as Spec R. Quasi-compactness and
   lem-sfb-qc-space-affine-etale-atlas give an affine surjective étale atlas Spec A → X.
   The chart test in def-sfb-space-morphism-properties and
   lem-weil-pbc-lft-affine-pairs make A a finite type R-algebra. Fix D generators.
   For empty X use the empty atlas and D = 0.
2. Over any test scheme B, restrict around v to Spec C. The affine fibre-product
   formula lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra identifies an atlas point w with
   a prime of A ⊗[R] C. The residue/localization formulas of
   def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime identify κ(w) with the fraction field of its
   quotient domain. It is generated as a field over κ(v) by the D algebra generators.
   Every fraction x/y is algebraic over the subalgebra generated by them, using yT-x.
   Apply lem-sfb-algebraic-adjoin-transcendence-bound to obtain trdeg ≤ D.
3. For an arbitrary étale chart U of the base change, pull back the atlas and then
   form its common refinement with U, using lem-trf-space-fibre-products-etale-covers.
   Lift u to a point t of the refinement, whose atlas image is w. Both projections
   are étale and the projection to U is surjective. By def-weil-etale-morphism and
   lem-weil-pbc-etale-algebra-residue-field-separable, κ(t) is algebraic over both
   κ(u) and κ(w), compatibly over κ(v).
4. The two applications of lem-kun-transcendence-degree-tower identify the relative
   transcendence degrees of κ(u), κ(t), and κ(w). Step 2 gives the bound. The choice
   of D preceded B, U and u; empty fibres have no points and impose no condition. -/
theorem exists_universal_fibre_transcendence_bound
    (Y : Over (Spec (CommRingCat.of (ULift.{u} ℤ)))) [IsAffine Y.left]
    (X : AlgebraicSpace (Spec (CommRingCat.of (ULift.{u} ℤ))))
    (f : X ⟶ schemeSpace Y) (hf : MapIsFiniteType f)
    (hqs : MapIsQuasiCompact (pullback.diagonal f.hom.hom)) :
    ∃ D : ℕ, ∀ (B : Over (Spec (CommRingCat.of (ULift.{u} ℤ)))) (g : B ⟶ Y)
      (U : (enlargedFibreProduct f (dimensionFlatBaseMap g)).EtaleSite)
      (x : (enlargedFibreProduct f (dimensionFlatBaseMap g)).etaleForget.obj U),
      @Algebra.trdeg (B.left.residueField ((dimensionFlatChartMap f g U).left x))
        (((enlargedFibreProduct f (dimensionFlatBaseMap g)).etaleForget.obj U).residueField x)
        _ _ (((dimensionFlatChartMap f g U).left).residueFieldMap x).hom.toAlgebra ≤
          (D : Cardinal.{u}) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace
